# Committing in this repo

Standing grant from Spencer on 2026-08-06: commit without asking each time.
This replaces the older default of drafting a message and stopping. Pushing and
opening PRs are not included, so do those only when he says to.

Three conditions, all mandatory.

## 1. Right branch

Check `git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD` before every commit. Branches are named
for their ticket, for example `spencer/BESS-2505-stack-light`, and a branch holds
that one ticket's work only.

Never commit to `main`. If the branch does not match the work you just did,
stop and say so rather than committing anyway. If the change spans two tickets,
stop and let him pick the branch.

Cut every ticket branch from `main`. Never stack one on an open PR branch.
"Merge main" into a PR whose base is not `main` means merge its base. Check
`gh pr view --json baseRefName` first. On PR 3228 main added 17 stray commits.

Never switch the branch of a checkout another agent may be using. Cut the new
ticket branch in its own worktree.

Stage deliberately by path. Never use `git add -A` or `git add .` in this repo:
the tree carries a lot of untracked noise (`__pycache__`, `test/.venv`,
`.pytest_cache`) that must not land in a commit. Commit only files that belong
to the current ticket.

The `rw-test-infrastructure` submodule is its own repo and its own PR, merged
before the pointer bump. Do not bundle a submodule change into a parent commit.

A pin bump is the pointer change only. Run no driver or combiner suites and
keep the PR tiny unless he asks.

Never merge while any check is pending, required or not.

## 2. Never co-authored, never attributed

Never add a `Co-Authored-By` trailer to anything, for any reason. The repo
history has zero of them and it stays that way. Spencer is the sole author on
the record.

Nothing in a commit, PR title, or PR body may mention AI, Claude, Cursor, an
assistant, or being generated. No "Generated with" footer, no robot emoji, no
tool attribution anywhere in the
subject, body, or trailers.

After committing, verify with `git log -1 --format="%an <%ae>%n%b"` and confirm
the author is Spencer and no trailer or attribution slipped in.

## 3. Message reads like Spencer wrote it

Commit subject and PR title share one shape: `prefix: summary`, matching CI's
regex `^(?=.{1,75}$).+: \w.+$` from `.github/pr-title-checker-config.json`.
Lowercase area prefix (`eol:`, `hil:`, `site:`, `docs:`, `pacman:`), one space,
then a word character. Hard maximum 75 characters including the prefix. Count
them, do not eyeball it. No ticket designator in the summary.

Squash-merge takes the final commit summary from the PR title, so the PR title
has to pass the same check even when the branch commit is already right.

His own commits are the model for voice:

```
eol: settle cal power points on convergence, not a fixed dwell
eol: carry the GUI's readable test names into MES rows
```

Plain and specific, says what changed and often why, lowercase, no filler, none
of the padding words in `personal-writing-style.mdc`. Do not restate the diff as
a bullet list. Add a body only when there is a real reason worth recording, and
write it in full sentences. The whole message, subject included, is 50 words at
most. Count them.

## One commit, never force push

Hold work uncommitted until the task is done, run lint and tests, then make one
commit. No commit per sub-change.

Never force push: not `--force`, not `--force-with-lease`, not on his own
branch. Work on an already-pushed branch goes on as a new commit; squash-merge
collapses it anyway. Push only when he says to, as a fast-forward. If a push is
rejected as non-fast-forward, stop and tell him. "Fix it" on an open PR's
review comment means commit and push.

On PR 2386 an amended, force-pushed commit left GitHub's PR pointing at the old
SHA for over ten minutes. A merge in that window would have taken the wrong
commit.

# PR bodies

Flat statements of fact in his voice. A reviewer should learn what changed, at
which addresses and files, and what is still unverified. Nothing else.

Order: what the PR adds, the decisions worth recording, `## Test plan`, then the
ticket key and its Jira URL on the last two lines. Nothing else follows the test
plan. Hard wrap at 80 columns.

Open with what the PR adds. Never frame it as a missing test or a regression
that would go unseen.

Open every PR with `gh pr create --draft`. He decides when it is ready for
review. Do not re-draft or otherwise change a PR that is already open.
"Make the PR" is the go to push and open the draft. Raise a concern in the
same reply. Do not hold the PR or ask for a second go.

Name real things: register addresses, file paths, function names, counts. "Six
staging words at 42043, then Update Time at 42042" over "the required write
sequence".

Never link, cite, or mention another PR in the body, by URL or by number.
That includes stacked PRs: GitHub already shows the base branch. Write `#N`
only for a PR, since GitHub links it. A CI run is "run 85", not "#85".

## Length

Short. A reviewer should finish it in under a minute.

Hard budget for the whole body: 200 words. Count it before posting, do not
eyeball it. `gh pr view <n> --json body --jq '.body' | wc -w` after posting, or
`wc -w` on the file before. Over budget means cut a section, not rewrap the
lines. A body you touch gets cut to budget, even if it was over before.

Inside that budget:

- What the PR adds: three or four lines, naming the file.
- Decisions: at most four bullets, one line each, and only for a choice a
  reviewer would otherwise question. A decision needing two lines belongs in
  the commit body instead.
- Test plan: one line per item, evidence in the clause, never a sub-paragraph.

Skip the investigation, the theories that did not pan out, and any explanation
of how the system works that the reviewer already knows.

If a paragraph is not changing whether someone approves the PR, cut it.

## Cut every flourish

No rhetoric, no persuasion, no narrating your own work, no closing line that
restates the paragraph above it. If a sentence exists to make the change sound
considered, delete it.

```
❌ Commissioning is where every unit already gets touched, so this is the
   place to fix it.
❌ **Worth a look**: the commit is queued unconditionally.
❌ - [ ] Real inverter. The only step that counts.
✅ The commit is queued unconditionally behind the staging write, where the
   PyQt tool gates it on the staging write succeeding.
✅ - [ ] Real inverter
```

## Test plan and honesty

`- [x]` only for what you ran, with the command and the result: "`make lint` and
`make test` in `dynapoweremu`: clean, 278 pass". `- [ ]` for what you did not,
named plainly and left unexplained. Pre-existing lint or CI noise gets one clause
saying it predates the branch and was left alone. Leave out a run you already
told him was not needed.

State an unverified thing as a fact and stop. "Whether each stack's event log
stamps off the Domain clock is unresolved" is finished. Do not build a paragraph
around it or apologize for it.

# PR comments

Plain text in his voice, a few sentences. No headers or bold. One line per
paragraph so GitHub flows it; the 80 column wrap is for bodies only. Never
reply to Copilot review threads.

# Jira and anything else under his name

The same budget discipline applies to Jira comments and ticket descriptions:
the facts, then stop.

Declarative statements only. A question in his voice reads as Spencer asking
his team something he never asked. Turn an open item into a status line: "Not
merging until the MES API accepts the field", not "Can someone confirm?". Do not
address teammates by name with requests. Raise open questions with Spencer in
chat.

- Findings go in the ticket description, not a comment.
- Link every ticket, PR, CI run, and attachment you name.
- Attach a long list, like affected serials, as a file. Do not paste it.
- No planning labels such as "Gap 1 (week 1)" in a summary.
- A link edits both tickets. Touch only the tickets he named or reported.
- An unneeded ticket gets deleted, not closed as Won't Do.
- A new ticket is a Sub-task under its story, never a Story or Task on the
  epic.
  Pick the story by the area the change touches, such as PacMan EOL or
  Gateway EOL, not the ticket the conversation started from.
- Fix a misfiled ticket by moving it. Never file a replacement.
